<p>You can learn more about that project from <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VqtEppZmjfw">the TED Talk just above</a> by its director, the late William Noel of Baltimore’s Walters Art Museum. Thanks to his dedication and that of his team, we now have writings of Archimedes like the <em>Stomachion</em>, which deals with a kind of children’s puzzle that turns out to have considerable similarities with an&nbsp;early problem in the field of combinatorics, and more importantly, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Method_of_Mechanical_Theorems"><em>The Method of Mechanical Theorems</em></a>, an explanation of calculus-like procedures for determining area or volume. None of this proves that Archimedes was the inventor of combinatorics or calculus, exactly, but it does show us just how much further he’d managed to push Greek mathematics than we’d previously known. Besides, any work that can bring together historians, classicists, mathematicians, conservators, physicists, and computer scientists more than two millennia after its composition surely merits a place in the canon.</p>
